      "id": 1,
      "text": "Granite was named for the granite found in the nearby mountains when a post office was established in 1889.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=GR008"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Originally part of Greer County, Texas, Granite became part of Oklahoma Territory in 1896 and Oklahoma in 1907.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.okhistory.org/publications/enc/entry?entry=GR008"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Headquarters Mountain rises 350 feet directly above the north side of Granite, reaching an elevation of 1,980 feet.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Granite%2C_Oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Lake Altus on the North Fork Red River is located just two miles east of the center of Granite.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
